<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Qustul</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Qustul is an archaeological site in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-nubia">Lower Nubia</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> near the modern border between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a>, now under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-nasser">Lake Nasser</a>. Its Cemetery L held large A-Group tombs of the late 4th millennium BCE with Egyptian goods, including an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/incense">incense</a> burner with royal imagery that some scholars took as evidence of early Nubian kingship. Later it held royal tombs of the Nobadian kingdom.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/qustul.jpg" alt="Qustul" width="320" height="276" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Daderot · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Qustul_Incense_Burner,_Qustal,_Nubia,_Cemetery_L,_tomb_24,_A-Group,_3200-3000_BC,_limestone_-_Oriental_Institute_Museum,_University_of_Chicago_-_DSC08007.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
